5.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=4x-{x}^{2}$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "133450b7-1b39-5639-acd0-5a1f9b4407fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536810 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=4x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=2$ and $x=4.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=4x-{x}^{2}$ between the lower limit $x=2$ and the upper limit $x=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{2}^{4}4x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536823 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536823 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536823 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536823 #edb/ref 1071 "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536823 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "328da786-31ee-5734-9605-611059a862b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536823 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x+3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-1}{n}=\\frac{6}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{6}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{6i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\frac{2}{(1+6i/n)+3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\frac{2}{6i/n+4})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\,\\frac{1}{3i/n+2}\\,)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536829 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c8de7d3-85f5-5987-bc43-0d9881c2dbaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536829 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=8{x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8{(\\,2+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536831 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bc9664f-fb9e-55ce-8ee2-e0e93f2cfcb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536831 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4},$ the lower bound is $a=-4$ and the upper bound is $b=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-1-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536837 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536837 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536837 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536837 #edb/ref 1071 "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a9d714c-fb16-5bf1-a044-d2aaa53f540d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536837 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=3{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3{(\\,1+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536852 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8aa630f2-bd34-5568-baf8-ea73abca85e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536852 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536861 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94a24590-9d5e-597a-b9fc-4e27a31e8bcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536861 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+8)^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{((1+\\frac{3i}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,9+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536862 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8b242c0012bdbc048f350158333b78e9484d716aa44f28511df2d7126dd02098.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b711075-e236-581f-bbc6-a977cb1be5c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536862 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
